            "Emergency nursing is the care of people whose lives or limbs are threatened in the next minutes to hours — the motorbike rider carried in from the Kumasi–Accra road, the toddler who drank kerosene from a soft-drink bottle, the woman who collapsed in the market, the farmer bitten by a carpet viper, the man whose chest pain started an hour ago, the child with cholera who cannot sit up. In Ghana most of these patients do not arrive by ambulance: they come in taxis, on the back of a motorbike or carried by relatives, often after hours of delay at home, at a prayer camp or with a traditional healer, and frequently to a <strong>health centre or CHPS compound</strong> where the nurse is the first and sometimes the only trained person present. The outcome is decided less by what the surgeon does later than by what the first nurse does in the first hour: an open airway, oxygen, pressure on a bleeding wound, a litre of Ringer's lactate, a dose of adrenaline, a correct triage decision and a fast, well-communicated referral.",
            "Ghana's emergency system has grown quickly. The <strong>National Ambulance Service</strong> is reached on the toll-free number <strong>112</strong> (the Ghana National Fire Service is 192 and the Police 191), and since 2020 every constituency has had at least one equipped ambulance with trained emergency medical technicians. The <strong>Accident and Emergency units</strong> of the teaching hospitals (Korle Bu, Komfo Anokye, Cape Coast, Tamale, Ho) and the regional hospitals receive referrals; Komfo Anokye has trained emergency physicians since 2009 and emergency nursing is now a recognised specialty. But the referral ladder still begins at the periphery, and the district hospital is where most emergency surgery, blood transfusion and caesarean sections actually happen. Knowing what your facility can and cannot do — whether it has oxygen at night, blood in the fridge, a functioning suction machine, a doctor on call, a vehicle — is part of emergency nursing, because the decision to stabilise and move is often the most important one you make.",
