            "<strong>Triage</strong> (from the French <em>trier</em>, to sort) is a rapid, structured assessment that assigns every arriving patient a priority category so that the limited staff, beds and equipment reach the people who will die or lose a limb without them first — not the person who arrived first, shouted loudest, knows the matron or has the most money. It is done at the door, by an experienced nurse, in two or three minutes, with a brief look, a few questions and a set of vital signs; it is <strong>not a diagnosis</strong> and it is <strong>not final</strong>: a patient's category is reviewed at intervals and changed whenever they get worse or better. Most Ghanaian emergency units now use the <strong>South African Triage Scale (SATS)</strong>, which combines a <strong>Triage Early Warning Score</strong> built from mobility, respiratory rate, heart rate, systolic blood pressure, temperature, level of consciousness and the presence of trauma with a list of <strong>discriminators</strong> — findings that place a patient in a high category regardless of the score (for example active seizure, severe pain, a threatened airway, a stab to the chest, a burn over a large area, a pregnant woman with abdominal pain or bleeding, hypoglycaemia). The colours and target times are in the table below. A simpler four-colour scheme — red, yellow, green and black — is used at the scene of a mass casualty incident and is covered in the final module.",
