          paragraphs: [
            "A set of observations is only as good as its technique. Count the respiratory rate for <strong>30 seconds while appearing to take the pulse</strong>, so the patient does not alter it. Take the pulse <strong>manually</strong> for 30 seconds, or a full minute if it is irregular, and record the rhythm and volume, not just the rate from the oximeter. Use the <strong>right cuff size</strong> — a cuff that is too small reads high, too large reads low — on a supported arm at heart level, and measure lying and standing when postural hypotension is suspected (a fall of 20 mmHg systolic or 10 mmHg diastolic on standing, or symptoms). Read the <strong>oxygen saturation</strong> only when the probe shows a good waveform or a steady signal; cold hands, nail varnish, shock, movement and bright light produce false readings, and a saturation of 95% means nothing if the patient is working hard to achieve it on oxygen. Record the <strong>temperature</strong> by the same route each time and remember that hypothermia (under 36 °C) is as much a sign of sepsis as fever. Assess the <strong>conscious level</strong> by AVPU or the Glasgow Coma Scale, and note <strong>new confusion</strong> — in an older person it is often the only sign of sepsis, hypoxia or a drug effect. Add <strong>urine output</strong>, <strong>pain</strong>, <strong>blood glucose</strong> where relevant and, in the pregnant woman, the fetal heart. Write the observations on the chart yourself, at the time, in the right patient's chart — recording a set in the wrong chart, or signing for observations that were never done, is a patient safety incident and a professional offence, not a clerical error.",
            "<strong>Early warning scores</strong> turn a set of observations into a number that anyone can act on. The most widely used is <strong>NEWS2</strong>: points are given for the respiratory rate, oxygen saturation (with a separate scale for patients with chronic lung disease whose target is 88–92%), whether the patient is on supplemental oxygen, systolic blood pressure, pulse, consciousness (with new confusion counting the same as responding only to voice) and temperature, and the total determines the response — a low score means routine four- to six-hourly observations; a score of 5 or more, or 3 in any single parameter, means urgent review by a clinician and hourly observations; a score of 7 or more means an emergency response and continuous monitoring. Many Ghanaian hospitals use a modified version, and obstetric (MEOWS) and paediatric (PEWS) charts use ranges adjusted for pregnancy and age. The score is a trigger, not a replacement for judgement: it must be <strong>calculated and acted on</strong> after every set of observations, the <strong>trend</strong> across sets matters as much as the total (a score rising from 1 to 5 over three sets is an emergency even if each value looks tolerable), and the nurse who feels that a patient with a normal score is 'not right' — the sensation experienced nurses describe as the patient looking grey, quieter, different — is required by most escalation policies to escalate on that concern alone, and is usually right.",
          ],
